#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088524} :activity/id #uuid "e07e7ccb-9045-51ac-ad31-1220ff860794" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049125 :topic/id #uuid "dcaddd67-a2eb-5a06-88ab-be47014dbd14" :topic/math-academy-id 863} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088893 :tutorial/content "Given the scalar $a,$ the **multiplicative inverse** of $a$ is defined as the scalar $a^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot a^{-1} = a^{-1} \\cdot a = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, the multiplicative inverse of $2$ is the number $2^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ because \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 =  2\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **inverse** of $A$ is defined as the $n \\times n$ matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^{-1} = A^{-1} \\cdot A = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the following $2\\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe inverse of the above matrix is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo verify that the above matrix is in fact the inverse of $A,$ first observe that if we multiply $A$ by the above matrix $A^{-1},$ then we get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1 & -1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply $A^{-1}$ by $A,$ then we also get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A} & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe do not yet know how to find the inverse of a matrix. However, we can verify that one matrix is the inverse of the other by carrying out the same procedure as we did here." :tutorial/id #uuid "427bce08-8aa6-508f-969b-2a2ed4eb8c7a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3043 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "390345b1-ff60-5803-bf48-ae86115b2de2" :step/math-academy-id 16452 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087506 :question/id #uuid "4f4be86f-5a12-5fdf-b98e-2095b1faae8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4499"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b9ef325-2358-5041-8a8d-b459e8ed9f09" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338996}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "42dd802f-22c6-5586-8fae-028b66defc18" :step/math-academy-id 16453 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087507 :question/id #uuid "7ab2814b-dc93-569b-bdd6-618880da6e2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4500"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75c67e5-3469-5730-9181-54df4ec6d5b2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348162}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "3941120f-c21f-51b0-b616-5b3059c55fc7" :step/math-academy-id 16454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088894 :tutorial/content "The number $0$ does not have an inverse for multiplication over the real numbers, because there's no such number $a$ that $a \\cdot 0 = 1.$ Instead, for every $a$ we have $a \\cdot 0 = 0.$\n\nSimilarly, not every matrix has an inverse.\n\n- If a matrix is not square, an inverse does not exist.\n\n- Some square matrices do not have an inverse. If a square matrix does not have an inverse, then it is said to be **singular**. An example of a singular matrix is the zero matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe will soon learn how to calculate the inverse of a square matrix, and we'll also soon learn how to determine when a square matrix does not have an inverse." :tutorial/id #uuid "fea084a4-999a-5a17-8f35-e8d7e5dbf25c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3044 :tutorial/title "Not Every Matrix Has an Inverse"} :step/id #uuid "46575a16-9bf0-5f72-8c33-a6d4bf578d86" :step/math-academy-id 16456}] :activity/title "Introduction to the Inverse of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087930}]}
